  "criteria": [
    {
      "detail": "every statutory value carries its provision, and every square is computed twice, once as a ratio of the four cell factors and once from the written-out closed form, which share no helper. Unsourced values: none. Squares where the two paths differ: none of 14.",
      "kind": "instrument",
      "name": "B122-1",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "detail": "the rate is written per input and the deduction ratio per class, and neither provision names the other's index; a buyer (in the Japanese arm, a seller) can cross between classes, and the crossing applies to every input at once, so its cost cancels between two positions and the square is a loop. Violations: none.",
      "kind": "premise",
      "name": "B122-2",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "detail": "the coefficients the tax agency prints in Q130 (110 分の 7.8 for the standard rate, 108 分の 6.24 for the reduced rate) are read back from this table's rates as v/(1+v) x 78/100, the national share. Checked on 10 rows; mismatches: none.",
      "kind": "known_answer",
      "name": "B122-3",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "detail": "the registered reading is that the statutory term log(1 + v(1 - r)) does not separate, so squares with two distinct rates and two distinct ratios are non-zero, exactly. CN-2026 9/9 non-zero, 4 independent, logs -0.027909, -0.036040, -0.063949, 0.001506, 0.002011, 0.003517, 0.029414, 0.038051, 0.067465; JP-2023-10-01..2026-09-30 1/1 non-zero, 1 independent, logs -0.003929; JP-2026-10-01..2028-09-30 1/1 non-zero, 1 independent, logs -0.005842; JP-2028-10-01..2030-09-30 1/1 non-zero, 1 independent, logs -0.009569; JP-2030-10-01..2031-09-30 1/1 non-zero, 1 independent, logs -0.013170; JP-2031-10-01.. 1/1 non-zero, 1 independent, logs -0.018349.",
      "kind": "own_reading",
      "name": "B122-4",
      "passed": true
    },
    {
      "detail": "the bare scalar field puts one cost on one input whoever buys it. Read: 16 of 16 class gaps are non-zero; largest 0.122218 in log. A seller who offset the statutory wedge by charging the disadvantaged class less would restore one cost only by writing a price that depends on the buyer's class and the input's rate together, which is itself a two-index schedule.",
      "grid": "B",
      "kind": "rival",
      "name": "B122-5",
      "passed": true,
      "rival_entailment": "phi carries no class index, so the ratio of two classes' costs is one on every input",
      "rival_premise": "cost(j, a) = phi(j): a price is a scalar on positions, one input has one cost whoever buys it",
      "rival_shape": "point prediction of zero"
    },
    {
      "detail": "the separable rival lets the class enter the cost as a factor, provided it is the same factor on every input: every symmetric class-level friction, every class markup, and every published class-only conversion constant has this form, and it makes every square exactly one. Read: 14 of 14 squares differ from one, decided in exact rational arithmetic with no tolerance.",
      "grid": "B",
      "kind": "rival",
      "name": "B122-6",
      "passed": true,
      "rival_entailment": "the ratio of two classes' costs is exp(psi(a) - psi(b)) on every input, so the ratio of that ratio across two inputs, which is the square, is one",
      "rival_premise": "log cost(j, a) = phi(j) + psi(a): the class changes the cost by one proportion on every input",
      "rival_shape": "point prediction of zero"
    },
    {
      "detail": "each carrier's law, per-input rates, per-class ratios and class edge are printed into the record with their provisions. Missing: none; carriers: 6.",
      "kind": "bookkeeping",
      "name": "B122-7",
      "passed": true
    }
  ],
